HLS ownership across API workers on one Linux host
==================================================

All HLS start, metadata, heartbeat, playlist, segment and stop requests go
through one elected media owner. Only that process owns native Process handles.
Every HTTP continuation still checks current organization, camera existence,
site and live/playback authority before media access or session mutation.

Election uses a stable private flock file; it is never unlinked. Control uses a
pathname Unix socket in the shared media directory, with mode0600, descriptor
based directory checks and same-UID peer verification on both ends. Messages,
connections and request time are bounded. Only whitelisted operations and flat
results cross this socket. Credentials may travel in the private start request,
but never in session-info responses, caller-visible errors or application logs.
A submitted mutation is not automatically replayed after an uncertain reply.
Same application UID is the trust boundary, not isolation from compromised code
running with that UID. Do not expose the socket or volume to untrusted services.

The base API mounts hls_media at /tmp/freesdn-hls. The single-host HA api-2
inherits that mount. Both development and production images precreate the path
as UID/GID1001, mode0700, so a fresh Docker named volume receives private ownership.
Custom deployments must give every cooperating API worker the same local volume
and application UID. An existing incorrectly owned volume is refused, not made
world writable. This directory contains derived media and private control files;
it is not customer configuration backup storage. Never remove it while workers
are running or place it on an unqualified network/distributed filesystem.

Shutdown refuses new local coordinator requests, closes the listener, cancels
accepted handlers, drains owned media children, removes its socket and releases
the lock last. A crashed owner releases the kernel lock; a surviving worker can
elect itself on a later request. Old session IDs then return not found. Clients
must start playback again. Linux parent-death binding separately kills native
children after owner death. Crash-left media files are not automatically removed.
A paused owner retains the lock; there is no unsafe lease-expiry takeover.

This is single-host coordination. It does not provide cross-host media routing,
NFS guarantees, seamless playback migration, individual viewer leases, aggregate
resource quotas or orphan-file retention. Bounded native diagnostics and child
cleanup remain described in HLS-STREAM-LIFECYCLE and CAMERA-CHILD-OWNERSHIP.

Regression tests use actual local workers and children with explicit synthetic
camera, authority and media fixtures. They do not prove HTTP authentication,
database policy or vendor RTSP compatibility. Packaged container qualification
and complete release/runtime evidence must pass separately before publication.
